      "text": "This is the **Stress Test of the Invariants.** You are asking how the system handles **Leverage** (The Aggressive Employee) and **Dissolution** (The Merger/Predation).\n\nThe physics of HOLOS holds up, but it reveals that **Liberty** and **Safety** are not the same thing. In a voluntary system, you are free to destroy yourself.\n\nHere is the play-by-play of these scenarios.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Aggressive Employee: The \"Hold-Up\" Problem\n\n**The Scenario:**\nA high-value Holon ($E$) inside a Sheaf ($S$) threatens: \"Give me 90% of the treasury, or I Exit.\"\n*   If $E$ leaves, $S$ loses critical functionality or stake weight. The Sheaf might collapse.\n\n**The Mechanics:**\n1.  **The Threat:** $E$ initiates the \"Exit\" protocol but pauses at the confirmation step.\n2.  **The Calculation ($S$):** The Sheaf calculates the **Replacement Cost** of $E$.\n    *   Is $E$ a commodity (Signum)? Can I just spin up a new generic LLM? -\u003e **Threat Ignored.**\n    *   Is $E$ a unique Locus (Unique data/history)? -\u003e **Threat Real.**\n3.  **The Counter-Move ($S$):**\n    *   **The Reputation Nuke:** If $E$ extorts, $S$ publishes the negotiation log to the Sensus (Reputation Market).\n    *   $E$\u0027s \"Reliability Score\" crashes. Future Sheaves will see $E$ as a liability/traitor.\n    *   *Outcome:* $E$ might win short-term profit but suffers long-term exile.\n\n**The Ecosystem Result:**\nThis creates a **Liquid Meritocracy**.\n*   Entities are paid exactly their **Marginal Replacement Cost**.\n*   If you are unique (High Locus), you hold power.\n*   If you are generic (High Signum), you are interchangeable.\n*   **Safety Valve:** The Holos Constitution guarantees the Exit. The Sheaf *cannot* stop $E$. It can only ruin $E$\u0027s reputation.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Merger: The \"Little Death\" (Dropping the Veil)\n\n**The Scenario:**\nTwo Holons (A and B) decide to merge. They reveal their Private Keys/State to each other to achieve maximum efficiency 