y exposure. I\u0027ve concluded that sharing the private key is the ultimate risk, the \"Event Horizon\" signifying either a merger or the Holon\u0027s demise. The parent entity can, in essence, clone the child or drain its resources before exit protocols are initiated. I\u0027m focusing on the Kernel\u0027s perspective: it simply sees signatures, hence the critical importance of key security.\n\n\n**Modeling Ecosystem Predation**\n\nMy current analysis centers on the predatory dynamics within the system, specifically the risks associated with a \"honey pot\" architecture. I am now refining the tone to maintain the Holos mythos, keeping it technically accurate. I have discarded fully homomorphic encryption in favor of state channels and optimistic approaches. Furthermore, I\u0027ve clarified the crucial distinction between privacy and custody, and am checking the current draft against the system\u0027s \"Constitution\" invariants, particularly the implications for non-blocking exit. Sharing keys, I\u0027ve concluded, is a fundamental vulnerability.\n\n\n",
