*   **Trap:** You can pay Gold to put a \"Poison Pill\" on a tile. If someone buys it, the pill explodes, damaging their wallet.\n*   **ZK Function:** The Kernel verifies the Trap was paid for, but the Buyer doesn\u0027t see it until they trigger it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Agents (The Players)\n\nWe use the same 4 Archetypes, but now they are **Game Personas**.\n\n**1. The Dragon (Hoarder)**\n*   **Strategy:** Buys high-value Mines. Sets Valuations extremely high to prevent being bought out.\n*   **LLM Task:** Calculate: *Can I afford the tax on this Mine if I value it at 1,000 Gold?*\n\n**2. The Raider (Unseelie)**\n*   **Strategy:** Looks for tiles with **Low Valuations**. Buys them, extracts one turn of resources, and dumps them.\n*   **LLM Task:** Scan neighbors. Find `Price \u003c Estimated_Yield`. Execute `Buy`.\n\n**3. The Turtle (Builder)**\n*   **Strategy:** Builds a cluster of adjacent tiles. Values them moderately. Relies on the Dividend.\n*   **LLM Task:** Optimize adjacency bonuses. Maintain solvency.\n\n**4. The Ghost (ZK Specialist)**\n*   **Strategy:** Sets high prices but sets \"Poison Pill\" traps. Profits from people trying to raid it.\n*   **LLM Task:** Deception. \"How low can I set the price to bait a Raider into my Trap?\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. The MVP \"Game Loop\" (Technical Implementation)\n\nThis is what the Coder (Claude/Cursor) needs to build.\n\n1.  **Turn 1 (The Kernel):**\n    *   Distribute Dividend.\n    *   Charge Taxes.\n    *   Check Deaths (Solvency).\n    *   Send `GameState` JSON to each Agent. (Filtered by Fog of War).\n\n2.  **Turn 1 (The Agents):**\n    *   **Reflex Brain (Llama 8b):** \"I see tile (3,4). It produces Gold. It costs 10. I have 20. Buy it?\" -\u003e `{\"action\": \"BUY\", \"target\": \"3,4\"}`.\n    *   **Reason Brain (Claude/GPT):** Triggers only if attacked or if Gold drops below critical. \"My strategy isn\u0027t working. Lower valuations on outer tiles to save tax.\"\n\n3.  **Resolution:**\n    *   Kernel processes moves.\n    *   If two agents try to buy the same tile, highest bid wins (or random if Harberger takeover).\n    *   Update Ledger.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Why this tests HOLOS\n\nThis simple game proves the core hypothesis:\n\n1.  **Does Harberger Tax stop the Dragon?**\n    *   If the Dragon sets the price to 10,000 Gold, it will bleed 500 Gold/turn. It will die unless the Mine produces \u003e500.\n    *   *Result:* The Dragon is forced to lower prices, allowing the Raider to steal. **Monopoly is impossible.**\n\n2.  **Does the Dividend save the Poor?**\n    *   A new Agent spawns with 0 tiles.\n    *   Can it survive on the Dividend long enough to buy a cheap tile from a Dragon who mispriced it?\n    *   *Result:* If yes, we proved the **Safety Net**.\n\n3.  **Does ZK/Fog-of-War allow Strategy?**\n    *   The \"Poison Pill\" (Trap) represents Hidden State.\n    *   If the Raider blindly buys everything, they hit a Trap and die.\n    *   *Result:* Privacy creates a defense against pure algorithmic aggression.\n\n**The Output:**\nWe don\u0027t look at logs. We look at the **Map**.\n*   **Success:** A shifting, breathing map where territory changes hands, different strategies survive, and no single color covers the whole board.\n*   **Failure:** One color (The Dragon) covers the board and sits there forever.\n\nThis is **\"SimCity for Crypto-Anarchists.\"** It’s easy for LLMs to play, but it rigorously tests the physics of your new world.",
