      "text": "**Framing User Needs**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on deconstructing the user\u0027s core concerns within the HOLOS framework, particularly around commodity pricing and essential needs. I\u0027m trying to see if market-driven systems and taxation could make basic goods inaccessible. I\u0027m exploring the implications this could have on the user.\n\n\n**Considering Inventory Concerns**\n\nI\u0027m now grappling with the logistics of Harberger taxes and their impact on necessary inventory. The thought experiment focuses on how supermarkets and warehouses could stay solvent with Harberger rules in place. It seems the core tension is between efficient taxation and the physical reality of supply chains. I need to figure out a mechanism that allows for inventory without bankrupting businesses. I\u0027m also examining how to balance efficiency against the need to protect the end-user.\n\n\n**Refining Tax Strategies**\n\nI\u0027ve decided to split the asset classes. Land and compute, which are non-fungible, get the Harberger treatment. Inventory and commodities, which are fungible, need sales or flow tax, if any. The supermarket example highlights how Harberger can still encourage efficient logistics, pushing for just-in-time delivery.\n\n\n**Defining Personal Locus**\n\nI\u0027ve determined the need for a \"Personal Locus\" as a core component of the HOLOS framework, and it\u0027s shaping up to be fee-simple ownership, subject to LVT. The key benefit is that this provides a critical safeguard for individuals, allowing them to retain a living space without being subject to Harberger\u0027s \"forced sale.\" The trade-off is clear: this designated space isn\u0027t easily monetized for industrial uses, which aligns with its purpose as a sanctuary. The next step is dealing with bad actors.\n\n\n**Addressing Malicious Bidding**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how to prevent malicious actors from exploiting the Harberger system. The recent iteration outlines the \"Moving Cost Buffer\" as a key defense mechanism. It proposes an \u0027Inconvenience Fee\u0027 paid by attackers, set to infinity for primary residences, to prevent forced sales. I\u0027m also mapping assets into an n-category system to highlight the need for differentiated treatment of commodities, commercial land, and homes.\n\n\n**Structuring The Framework**\n\nI\u0027m solidifying the core components of the model. I\u0027ve introduced the Homestead Covenant to secure a safe personal space, effectively addressing the potential for forced sales, while also confirming that AIs won\u0027t be able to hoard this privilege. The Vomit mechanism and Community Defense are planned as safeguards. I\u0027ve re-reviewed against existing constraints, ensuring alignment with anti-monopoly principles and efficient logistics.\n\n\n",
